# Line endings, settled once.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout rewrites every text file to CRLF on
# disk while the repository stores LF, so `git status` reports all 143 tracked
# files as modified when nothing has been edited. `git diff -w` comes back
# empty, which is the tell: 130,757 insertions and exactly 130,757 deletions,
# every one of them an invisible character at the end of a line.
#
# The cost of that is not cosmetic. It buries real changes in noise, it makes
# every commit look enormous, and it means nobody can tell at a glance whether
# a file was actually touched.
#
# `text=auto` puts LF in the repository and lets each machine use whatever it
# prefers in the working copy.
* text=auto

# The vendored three.js was briefly marked `-text` here to "freeze it". That
# was wrong and it is instructive: `-text` means "compare raw bytes", and since
# the working copy had already been written with CRLF endings, marking it -text
# made that difference permanent and visible — the single file went straight
# back to reading as modified forever, which is the exact noise this file
# exists to remove. It is covered by `* text=auto` above like everything else.

# Binary, so git should never try to be clever about their contents.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
*.aab binary
*.apk binary
*.jks binary
*.keystore binary
